  "narrative": "The enrichment analysis clearly identifies steroid hormone metabolism as the dominant pathway, with \"Androgen and Estrogen Metabolism\" (KEGG map00150) achieving an FDR of 0.0 and a remarkable fold-enrichment of 1200.911. Six of the ten input metabolites — testosterone (C00535), androstenedione (C00280), estradiol (C00951), estrone (C00468), dihydrotestosterone (C03917), and dehydroepiandrosterone (C01227) — map to this pathway, representing a near-complete sampling of the canonical androgen–estrogen interconversion network.\n\nThe non-steroidal metabolites — N-acetylgalactosamine, coumarin, xanthine, and beta-mercaptoethanol — did not contribute to any enriched pathway at the current significance threshold, suggesting the treatment effect is specifically targeting steroidogenesis rather than unrelated xenobiotic or nucleotide metabolic branches.\n\n**Key drivers and verified relationships.** KEGG graph traversal confirms that androstenedione and testosterone are immediate neighbours in the reaction graph (1 hop), reflecting the 17β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase–catalysed interconversion that is a central node in androgen metabolism. Testosterone is further linked to estradiol (C00535 → C00951) via a three-step path through intermediates, consistent with aromatase-mediated conversion. The co-enrichment of DHEA, androstenedione, testosterone, and estradiol across multiple SMPDB entries — including \"17-Beta Hydroxysteroid Dehydrogenase III Deficiency,\" \"Aromatase deficiency,\" and \"Metabolism of steroid hormones\" (Reactome R-HSA-196071) — suggests that the treatment perturbs multiple enzymatic steps simultaneously, rather than a single rate-limiting step.\n\n**Biological significance.** Dysregulation of this axis is consistent with conditions involving altered sex hormone balance, such as polycystic ovary syndrome, androgen-secreting tumours, or therapeutic modulation of steroidogenesis. The simultaneous elevation or depletion of these six steroids points to a upstream perturbation — likely at the level of cytochrome P450 enzymes or 17,20-lyase — that cascades into downstream androgen and estrogen pools. The non-involvement of the four remaining metabolites argues against a broader metabolic shift and instead supports a selective impact on the steroidogenic axis.",
